{
  // context-email: the Cloudflare Email Worker behind <username>@context.lc.
  //
  // Mail addressed to a username lands in that person's personal context, whose
  // path *is* the username. Shared contexts have no ingestion address — nothing
  // to configure, nothing to disable — so everything from outside passes through
  // one accountable owner before anyone else can see it. See the block comment
  // in src/controlPlane.ts for why that is structural rather than a filter.
  //
  // Cloudflare Email Routing is live on context.lc — MX, SPF and DKIM are
  // verified in DNS — but until this Worker is deployed and bound to a routing
  // rule, nothing receives mail: every message to <name>@context.lc goes
  // nowhere.
  //
  // ── DEPLOYMENT IS NOT COMPLETE WHEN `wrangler deploy` SUCCEEDS ────────────
  //
  // Three things are configured in the Cloudflare dashboard, not here, and the
  // Worker is either wrong or useless without them.
  //
  // 1. ROUTE IT AS A CATCH-ALL, NOT AS PER-ADDRESS RULES.
  //
  //    Email Routing rejects mail for an address with no matching rule, before
  //    the Worker ever runs. If addresses were added one rule at a time, then
  //    Cloudflare's own behaviour would differ between a claimed name and an
  //    unclaimed one — and every refusal in src/index.ts would be uniform for
  //    nothing, because the enumeration oracle would live one layer above it.
  //    The catch-all action must be "Send to a Worker → context-email", so
  //    every address on the domain reaches the same code and gets the same
  //    answer.
  //
  // 2. AUTHENTICATION IS THE OPEN BLOCKER. READ BEFORE ANNOUNCING THE FEATURE.
  //
  //    src/auth.ts refuses any message whose topmost `Authentication-Results`
  //    header does not carry this exact authserv-id. That is what stops a
  //    sender forging their own SPF/DKIM/DMARC verdict — and it depends on our
  //    MX prepending its header above anything the sender wrote.
  //
  //    Cloudflare appears not to send that header. Its own documentation of
  //    Email Routing describes stamping `ARC-Authentication-Results` (RFC 8617)
  //    rather than `Authentication-Results` (RFC 8601), with the ARC instance
  //    tag ahead of the authserv-id:
  //
  //      ARC-Authentication-Results: i=1; mx.cloudflare.net; dkim=pass …
  //      https://blog.cloudflare.com/email-routing-subdomains/
  //
  //    That breaks ingestion twice over, and neither break is a wrong value
  //    here: src/mime.ts collects only headers named `authentication-results`,
  //    so the ARC header never reaches the verifier at all; and even handed to
  //    it directly, the leading `i=1` is read as the authserv-id and the header
  //    is rejected as malformed. Both are pinned as tests — see "the header
  //    Cloudflare Email Routing actually sends" in src/auth.test.ts.
  //
  //    So the authserv-id below is, as far as the documentation goes, the RIGHT
  //    value; it is simply compared against a header that never arrives. Every
  //    message is refused today, whatever it is set to.
  //
  //    DO NOT "fix" this by trusting the topmost ARC header. That header is
  //    forgeable by the sender exactly as the other one is, and
  //    cloudflare/workerd issue #6740 reports that Worker-delivered mail may
  //    carry NO authentication verdicts at all — in which case the only header
  //    present would be the attacker's own. The question is not answerable from
  //    documentation; it needs one real delivery.
  //
  //    This repository's rule is that a guard nobody has checked is not a
  //    guard. Before enabling ingestion: send one message through, capture the
  //    raw headers, confirm (a) which header carries the verdict, (b) that the
  //    authserv-id below is the one Cloudflare actually writes, and (c) that it
  //    appears above any header the sender supplied. Add the captured headers
  //    as a fixture in src/auth.test.ts. Until then this fails CLOSED — the
  //    Worker refuses everything rather than trusting a forgery.
  //
  // 3. VERIFY `OPERATIONS_MAILBOX` AS AN EMAIL ROUTING DESTINATION.
  //
  //    postmaster@ and abuse@ are forwarded there, never ingested. RFC 2142
  //    requires them to stay deliverable and CLAUDE.md records that as
  //    load-bearing. Cloudflare will not forward to an unverified destination,
  //    so an unverified address means those two mailboxes bounce.
  //
  // CUSTOM DOMAINS ARE NOT ATTACHED HERE, and neither is the email route.
  // Same convention as infra/router: attaching them requires the zone to
  // already be on Cloudflare DNS, which a CI deploy cannot do and should not
  // silently try to.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "context-email",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  // A stable past date rather than "today": a future-dated (or clock-skewed)
  // CI runner rejects a compatibility_date that resolves to the future.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",

  "vars": {
    // The Convex HTTP-actions origin: https://<deployment>.convex.site — the
    // `.site` host, NOT the `.cloud` one the client library uses.
    //
    // Public information (it is in every web client's network tab), so it is
    // fine in a public repository.
    "CONTROL_PLANE_URL": "https://clean-ptarmigan-116.convex.site",

    // The apex we accept mail for. A recipient on any other domain is refused
    // before anything else happens.
    "INGEST_DOMAIN": "context.lc",

    // The authserv-id in the `Authentication-Results` header we will believe.
    // See note 2 above — CONFIRM THIS AGAINST A REAL DELIVERY.
    "AUTH_SERVICE_ID": "mx.cloudflare.net",

    // DELIBERATELY EMPTY. postmaster@ and abuse@ are forwarded here; with it
    // unset they are refused and a loud `operations_mailbox_unset` is logged.
    // Set it to a verified Email Routing destination. Not a secret, but it is
    // a real mailbox on a real person's account, so it is left for whoever
    // deploys rather than committed.
    "OPERATIONS_MAILBOX": "",

    // Hard ceiling on a message this Worker will read. Capped again in code at
    // 5 MB regardless of what is set here.
    "MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES": "5000000",

    // Allow-list of native R2 binding names, for self-hosters whose control
    // plane returns a `r2-binding` provider. Empty for the product deployment,
    // whose tenants bring their own buckets over the S3 API.
    "NATIVE_BINDINGS": ""
  },

  // EMAIL_WORKER_SECRET is a secret, set with `wrangler secret put` and never
  // committed. It is NOT `GATEWAY_SECRET`: see the contract block in
  // src/controlPlane.ts for why the two are deliberately separate and what a
  // leak of this one would and would not buy.

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
